@title atx-conformance interop day kit|pinned commit <sha7>, <date>
================================================================================
This kit carries atx-conformance at commit
<sha>: <N> fixtures, and both reference
verifiers pass all <N>.
Running a verifier needs no network. Building one needs it once, to fetch
dependencies, so this kit carries prebuilt Go binaries for <platforms>.

Check before you run. This folder is a copy of the release at
<release URL>
Download SHA256SUMS from that page, not from this folder, and run
shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS here (Linux: sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS). Every line
must say OK, and every file here must be listed in it. With the GitHub CLI:
gh attestation verify <file> -R <owner/repo>

Run the fixtures, one command, from this folder:
  macOS or Linux:  open Terminal, type cd and a space, drag this folder onto
                   the window, press Enter, then:                  ./verify
  Windows:         open this folder, type cmd in the address bar, press
                   Enter, then:                                    verify.cmd
  Expected last line:  <expected last line>    exit code 0
@nof5   A FAIL line is a bug in a verifier or a fixture: tell us, see CONTACT.txt.
@f5 The verifiers print text from the files they read without escaping control
@f5 characters. When you run a file someone else wrote, pipe the output through
@f5 cat -v, or write it to a file and open that.

Every private key in this kit is a published test key, so anyone can sign a
credential these fixtures accept: a pass shows that a verifier agrees with a
fixture, never that OpenA2A issued a credential.
@nof5
A credential of your own:  ./verify my-fixture.json
To check a credential of your own, use one that is no longer live: an expired
one, with the fixture's clock set inside its validity period, or one from a
test issuer. An ATX works as a bearer credential until it expires. The
verifier needs no private key; never give it one.
A fixture is your credential plus the keys the verifier trusts and the verdict
you expect: copy fixtures/baseline-valid.json, put your credential in its atx
member, your issuer's DID in verifierState.trustedIssuers and its public key in
verifierState.publicKeys, and set verifierState.clockRfc3339. Field by field:
README.md and docs/fixtures.md.
@nof5
In this folder:
  bin/<os>-<arch>/atx-verify  the Go verifier, one static binary per platform
  fixtures/  MANIFEST.sha256  the fixtures and their SHA-256 pins
  verifiers/python/           the Python verifier; offline install: PYTHON.md
  conformance.json            what the suite covers; notCovered lists the rest
  CONTACT.txt                 who to tell, and the release this copy came from
  README.md                   the repository README at the pinned commit
